# Target C-II — the finite switching cusp is exactly one middle-layer diameter cut

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only exact structural sharpening
**Scope:** any consecutive pair in the audited laminar O--O interval-shear coordinates; quantitative corollary for the triple-small adjacent pair
**Dependencies:** `target_c_laminar_multitrain_interval_shear_2026-09-15.md`; audited Section 157 shore resultants; C65 full actual diameter visibility; straight-line diameter-thrackle theorem
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The finite-cusp reduction treats an active diameter row as **switching** when its shore
incidence differs on two consecutive selected O--O cuts.  The interval theorem gives a
stronger physical interpretation which was not used there: the switching rows are not
an arbitrary bounded subset of the diameter graph.  They are exactly the full
diameter-graph edge boundary of the one physical layer between the two cuts.

Let

\[
P=(\tau_1,\ldots,\tau_m)
\]

be a selected dual-path O--O family with layers

\[
R_0,R_1,\ldots,R_m
\]

from the exact laminar shear theorem.  Fix an internal boundary `i`, so the physical
middle layer is `R_i`, between `tau_i` and `tau_{i+1}`.  Let `H` be the complete actual
diameter graph and put

\[
B_i:=V(H)\cap R_i.
\]

Then:

### Theorem MC.1 — exact middle-layer cut identity

A diameter edge switches between columns `i` and `i+1` if and only if it has exactly
one endpoint in `B_i`.  Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal S_i
:=\{h:\varepsilon_{h,i+1}\ne\varepsilon_{hi}\}
=\delta_H(B_i).
}
\tag{MC.1}
\]

Thus every switching edge has one and only one loaded endpoint in the physical middle
layer `R_i`.

### Theorem MC.2 — exact signed reaction of the cusp cut

Orient every switching diameter edge from its endpoint in `B_i` toward its endpoint
outside `B_i`, and write that length-`D` vector as `d_h`.  For the audited Section 157
cut resultants

\[
F_j=A_j u_j
=\sum_h\beta_h\varepsilon_{hj}r_h,
\]

one has the exact vector identity

\[
\boxed{
F_{i+1}-F_i
=\sum_{h\in\delta_H(B_i)}\beta_h d_h.
}
\tag{MC.2}
\]

Consequently the switching multiplier quantity from the adjacent-cut theorem is
literally the KKT weight of this diameter cut:

\[
\boxed{
B_i^{\rm sw}
:=\sum_h\beta_h|\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}|
=\sum_{h\in\delta_H(B_i)}\beta_h.
}
\tag{MC.3}
\]

### Corollary MC.3 — the finite cusp is a bounded straight-line-thrackle cut

For the triple-small pair from
`target_c_adjacent_cut_triple_small_2026-09-15.md`,

\[
\boxed{|\delta_H(B_i)|<21200+o(1),}
\tag{MC.4}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in\delta_H(B_i)}\beta_h
<{21200\over D}+o(D^{-1}),
}
\tag{MC.5}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\left\|\sum_{h\in\delta_H(B_i)}\beta_h d_h\right\|
<21200+o(1).
}
\tag{MC.6}
\]

The active large-derivative rows in the finite opposite shear are **precisely** the
edges of this cut.  Since `H` is a straight-line diameter thrackle, any two members of
`delta_H(B_i)` with disjoint endpoints cross properly.  Every endpoint in `B_i` is a
loaded extreme vertex of the C65 force hull.

This replaces the previous abstract `O(1)` cusp set by one bounded interface cut in
the actual Woodall diameter graph.

---

## 1. Interval endpoints identify the physical middle layer

Use the exact layer index `ell(v)` from the laminar interval theorem.  Orient one actual
diameter edge `h=xy` so

\[
a:=\ell(x)\le b:=\ell(y).
\]

Its selected-cut support is exactly

\[
I_h=\{a+1,a+2,\ldots,b\},
\tag{MC.7}
\]

with one constant nonzero shore-incidence sign on this interval.  With the standard
lower-to-higher orientation that sign is `+1`, so

\[
\varepsilon_{hj}
=\mathbf 1_{\{a<j\le b\}}.
\tag{MC.8}
\]

Compare consecutive columns `i` and `i+1`.  Their indicators differ exactly in one of
two cases:

1. `b=i`: the interval ends at `i`; or
2. `a=i`: the interval starts at `i+1`.

In the first case the higher endpoint `y` lies in layer `R_i`.  In the second case the
lower endpoint `x` lies in layer `R_i`.  The other endpoint lies strictly outside that
layer.

Conversely, if exactly one endpoint of a diameter edge lies in `R_i`, then its two
layer indices are either `a<i=b` or `a=i<b`, so its interval ends or starts at the
boundary and the edge switches.  If both endpoints lie in `R_i`, then `a=b=i` and the
selected-cut interval is empty, so the edge does not switch.

C65 identifies every actual diameter endpoint with a loaded vertex of `H`.  Therefore
this is exactly the graph cut `delta_H(B_i)`, proving (MC.1).

A useful directional split is now canonical:

\[
\mathcal S_i^-:=\{h=xy:\ell(x)<\ell(y)=i\},
\]

\[
\mathcal S_i^+:=\{h=xy:\ell(x)=i<\ell(y)\}.
\tag{MC.9}
\]

The first family consists of diameter edges entering the middle layer from earlier
layers; the second consists of edges leaving it toward later layers.

---

## 2. Consecutive cut-resultant subtraction is the layer reaction

For the lower-to-higher orientation `r_h=p_y-p_x`, equation (MC.8) gives

\[
\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi}
=\begin{cases}
-1,&h\in\mathcal S_i^-,\\
+1,&h\in\mathcal S_i^+,\\
0,&h\notin\mathcal S_i.
\end{cases}
\tag{MC.10}
\]

If `h` is in `S_i^-`, then `r_h` points from the outside endpoint toward the middle
layer, so `-r_h` is the vector from the middle endpoint to the outside endpoint.  If
`h` is in `S_i^+`, then `r_h` already points from the middle endpoint outward.  Thus in
both cases

\[
(\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi})r_h=d_h.
\tag{MC.11}
\]

Subtract the exact Section 157 identities for the two consecutive cuts:

\[
\begin{aligned}
F_{i+1}-F_i
&=\sum_h\beta_h
 (\varepsilon_{h,i+1}-\varepsilon_{hi})r_h\\
&=\sum_{h\in\delta_H(B_i)}\beta_h d_h.
\end{aligned}
\]

This proves (MC.2).  Since every switch changes a `0/1` incidence by exactly one,
(MC.3) follows immediately.

The same identity can be read as equilibrium of the physical middle layer: the two
train-side contact resultants differ by exactly the net outward diameter reaction on
that layer.  No response-conductance inequality and no coefficient floor are used.

---

## 3. Insert the triple-small estimates

For the adjacent pair selected by the triple-small theorem, its switching count and
switching multiplier satisfy

\[
N_i<21200+o(1),
\qquad
B_i^{\rm sw}<{21200\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\]

By (MC.1) and (MC.3), these are exactly (MC.4)--(MC.5).  Every `d_h` has norm `D`, so
(MC.2) gives

\[
\|F_{i+1}-F_i\|
\le D\sum_{h\in\delta_H(B_i)}\beta_h
<21200+o(1),
\]

recovering the existing resultant estimate with a sharper structural interpretation.

The finite-cusp theorem classifies a row as large-derivative precisely when its
interval contains exactly one of the two adjacent coordinates.  By (MC.1), those rows
are exactly `delta_H(B_i)`.

---

## 4. Thrackle and Woodall localization

The complete actual diameter graph is a straight-line thrackle.  Therefore the finite
cusp cannot be an arbitrary collection of `<=21200` active constraints:

- every cusp edge joins one middle-layer loaded terminal to one loaded terminal outside
  the layer;
- two cusp edges with four distinct endpoints cross in their interiors;
- all cusp endpoints are convex-position loaded terminals of the C65 force hull;
- in the Woodall forest branch the cut sits inside a disjoint union of caterpillars;
- in the Woodall cyclic branch it sits inside the unique odd-unicyclic component with
  pendant leaves.

In particular, if `B_i` contains exactly one loaded terminal `z`, then

\[
\boxed{
\delta_H(B_i)=E_H(z),
}
\tag{MC.12}
\]

so the entire finite cusp is one actual diameter star.  More generally, internal
diameter edges with both endpoints in `B_i` are invisible to the cusp and every other
diameter incidence of a middle-layer terminal appears in it.

No stronger star conclusion is claimed without additional control of `|B_i|` or the
cyclic placement of the middle-layer terminals.

---

## 5. Interaction with the KKT curvature identity

For the finite opposite shear with coefficients `(1,-s)` and projective sign chosen so
`|u_i-su_{i+1}|=eta`, the exact KKT curvature identity gives

\[
Q(1,-s)
=\sum_h\beta_h|A_h|^2-A_i-A_{i+1}.
\]

The three row types and (MC.3) imply the useful upper bound

\[
\boxed{
Q(1,-s)
\le
B_i^{\rm sw}+\eta^2-A_i-A_{i+1}.
}
\tag{MC.13}
\]

Indeed cusp rows contribute exactly one to `|A_h|^2`, rows spanning both coordinates
contribute `|u_i-su_{i+1}|^2=eta^2`, and rows meeting neither contribute zero; the
total diameter multiplier is one.

For the triple-small pair,

\[
B_i^{\rm sw}=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad
\eta=O(D^{-1}),
\]

so

\[
Q(1,-s)
\le -A_i-A_{i+1}+O(D^{-1}).
\tag{MC.14}
\]

This does **not** close the branch: the current audited stack has no quantitative lower
bound on either `A_i` or `A_{i+1}`, and a negative KKT-weighted curvature by itself does
not control the individual first-order diameter cusps.  Equation (MC.14) records the
precise missing scalar rather than silently assuming the rejected response inequality.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The finite-cusp normal form can now be stated at source level as

```text
one physical middle layer R_i
+ two nearly parallel O-O train sides tau_i,tau_{i+1}
+ the complete active diameter interface is delta_H(B_i)
+ |delta_H(B_i)|=O(1)
+ beta(delta_H(B_i))=O(1/D)
+ every two disjoint interface chords cross
+ exact layer reaction F_{i+1}-F_i
+ opposite-shear weighted curvature <= -A_i-A_{i+1}+O(1/D).
```

A valid next theorem no longer needs to reason about an unstructured finite list of
cusp rows.  It can attack one bounded edge cut of a Woodall thrackle.  Three concrete
routes remain:

1. control the number/cyclic order of loaded terminals in `B_i` and classify the
   resulting thrackle cut;
2. prove a positive lower floor for `A_i+A_{i+1}` and combine it with a source-level
   (not merely KKT-average) finite replacement; or
3. exploit the exact layer interface to build a cardinality-preserving replacement
   that is allowed to break the two old train-side contact families.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only structural sharpening.  C-II and Level I remain
open.
